An expectation of boons and blessings. Things believed to be true before there is
evidence, written down so that when the evidence arrives it is clear which
beliefs it confirmed and which it demolished.

--

I believe that a shared pool three people argue over is more interesting than
three avatars three people drive. The whole project is that bet.

I believe the stalemate is real. That if you take a lane-pusher and subtract the
heroes, the jungle, and the items, what remains genuinely does not move, and the
phase-2 demo will show it plainly rather than being an anticlimax.

I believe that stamping a soldier's upgrades at birth, which was chosen because
it is fast, will turn out to be the best design decision in the project -- because
the delay it introduces is what makes a placement a bet, and a bet is what makes
a placement worth arguing about. Performance and design pointing the same way is
usually a sign that something is right.

I believe milestones will keep being the right answer to questions nobody has
asked yet. Every time this design has needed to compare two situations, the small
integer has been correct and the distance has been wrong.

I believe that one soldier record for everything that walks will hold, and that
the first time somebody wants to break it will be the first time the design has
gone wrong somewhere else.

I believe six people will find something degenerate in the first hour that none
of this documentation anticipated, and that it will be more interesting than
anything written down here.

